welcome everyone to new coffee break  here on my channel firm learning my name  is heinrich i'm a former mckinsey  consultant and on my channel i want to  help you to become successful in the  first years of your career and today i  want to share with you 10 tips to  communicate better and be perceived as  more senior on the job some of these  tips are quite sneaky quite juicy i can  promise you that all of them are highly  effective so let's get into it with tip  number one so sometimes from a colleague  or client you will hear an expression or  a concept that you don't know yet and  many people will just ask i don't know  this what does this mean can you please  explain this to me and of course this  implies that you have no idea about what  this is about you might be perceived as  not really knowledgeable so next time  this happens to you try instead to say  something like what does this expression  mean to you what do you understand with  this term because this implies that  actually you know what this expression  means but you even have this higher  level understanding that sometimes  different people understand different  things with the same expression so you  want to clarify it and this takes away  this notion that you have no idea what  you're talking about next step we all  know this we already have lots of things  to do with maybe already late in the  evening and now your supervisor your  leadership your team need approaches you  want you to do even more so what a  junior person might reply to this is  something like i just cannot do it i  have too many things to do can you  please ask someone else to help you with  that and of course this usually doesn't  give the best vibes it doesn't show the  best attitude so instead try saying  something like sure let's look into this  together can you please help me  re-prioritize my tasks for the rest of  the day because this shows that yes you  are willing to do it you just need the  help from the other person to decide  what the most important things are that  need to get done today this evening or  this week next tip a mistake that many  people do is that they end their  sentences always on a high pitch so they  go up with their voice at the end of  every sentence and this might look  something like this so you share an idea  and then this leads to this other thing  and actually you also have this other  thing to share and then there's also  this other aspect that's relevant  so always at the end of every sentence  the pitch goes up and this leaves the  people not really engaged this takes  away gravitas takes away importance from  the things that you say instead try to  end every sentence with your pitch of  your voice going down  so say something like this i have this  very important idea that today i want to  share with you it is really something  that will make a big contribution to the  work at the end of every sentence go  down with your pitch this just adds  gravitas and underlines the points that  are really important next step you might  be faced with the situation where you  really don't know what to do maybe you  get a task from your leadership but you  have no clue on how to address this on  how to structure this here again a more  junior person might just say i have no  clue i really don't know what to do i  feel completely lost can you please help  me with that and again of course this  often doesn't give the best impression  instead try to say something like i  would really appreciate your insights in  helping me shape this approach make it  about the other person make it about the  experience the insights of the other  person and now together you will  structure and solve this may be quite  challenging task next step is to focus  on organization and team leadership  questions  juniors in meetings often very much  focus on tasks on responsibilities on  things that need to get done well of  course that's very important as well one  tip i once got is that if you want to be  perceived as more senior ask more  questions regarding the organization the  team leadership as a leader you're  responsible for enabling your team for  making sure that your team is set up for  success so ask questions exactly in that  direction do we have the right  capabilities on the team is this person  able to lead the project in the right  way can this person actually lead and  supervise this more junior colleague are  we working together with the right  clients are we set up for success so  these are typical questions that most  senior people will ask try to do this  yourself if you want to be perceived  this way as well these were the first  five of the 10 tips i want to share with  you today if you took any value out of  them at all hit the like button to help  me with the algorithm and of course  subscribe and turn on the notification  bell to stay up to date with my content  but now let's continue with tip number  six and this is a mistake that i often  see more junior leaders do and this is  that whenever there's a meeting in front  of your own internal team or maybe also  when you talk to clients or other  external stakeholders this leader wants  to present him or herself the leader  will want to give the result give the  presentation by themselves and then  often the team is just sitting in the  back listening to this junior leader  present try to find a setup where you as  the leader will sit in the back and  actually your team presents maybe not  everything but at least some sub  chapters of the overpresentation of the  overall meeting put them in the  spotlight let them shine of course  everyone knows that you will not have  done everything yourself it's clear that  your team will have done a good part of  the work as well so give them that  exposure give them that spotlight and  this also makes you to be perceived as a  good people leader good people leader  who trusts in his or her team a people  leader who wants to develop his team  members this will be perceived as more  senior as way next let's say you want to  praise someone for their job for their  work and here many people will just say  hey you did a good job thumbs up great  work it's great what you've accomplished  well of course this is not bad it's not  wrong try to say something like  thank you for your leadership on this  initiative thank people for their  leadership this is something that i've  seen very often most senior people do  and this of course instills also the  idea in more junior colleagues that they  are not just here to get some work done  but they are here to lead and maybe just  one small initiative maybe just a sub  part of the overall project but you want  people to take ownership take  responsibility  lead forward make things work so stress  this with your words whenever you give  feedback and give praise to your  colleagues next many colleagues  especially more junior ones don't really  communicate that much the winner  proactively reach out to leadership to  share some points maybe in one-on-one  team meetings this is where might say  something what i've seen many project  leads do with companies like mckinsey  bcg bain is that at the end of every  week they will write an end of week  email sharing three things first the  highlights of the last week the low  lights of the last week and the  priorities for the next week and i will  send this to the whole team and their  leadership be a proactive communicator  share your thoughts be transparent also  things that haven't gone that great this  is what will make you be perceived as  more seniors right next step when  someone wants to communicate an update  on a project or any other initiative or  train of thought that they're having it  will often go like this  so yeah this is what i think and this is  because of this reason and then there's  also this other reason and ir this other  reason is important this way  so don't do that don't communicate like  this this will be perceived as very  unstructured instead leverage the rule  of three if you don't know what that is  i highly encourage you to watch another  video that i will insert somewhere above  here where i discuss this in detail but  the basic idea is that you should start  always by saying something like this is  what i hold true and it's because of  three reasons so you always start by  saying there are three reasons for  something first second and third very  easy structure very effective check out  the other video if you have no idea what  this might make sense next step don't  talk too fast and strategically use  breaks make use of silence many junior  people just talk far too fast talking  fast is usually perceived as being  nervous and not being self-secure  instead strategically use silence just  sit there and say nothing especially  after for instance a client interview  explained a couple of things and you  won't actually hear even more just say  nothing  because for many people this would be  uncomfortable and often will instill the  other person to share even more but now  i'd love to hear from you do you have  any other additional tips that you'd  like to share leave a comment with your  insights this would be highly  appreciated big big thanks to all the  members of the channel thanks so much  for your support my name is heinrich i  release weekly content here next to me i  will insert some new videos if you want  to continue your learning journey all  the best to you until next week and bye  bye

